  <div class="about-page">
    <h1>About</h1>

    <p>
      I write here to make sense of things that stay stuck in my head long after the day is over.
    </p>

    <p>
      Most of these pieces start in the quiet hours of the night, when the day's noise dies down and whatever you've been avoiding or noticing finally catches up to you. In your head, thoughts tend to feel deceptively solid or overly dramatic. Putting them into sentences is the only way I know to test whether an idea actually holds weight, or if it was just late-night restlessness disguised as insight.
    </p>

    <p>
      The name <em>Bedroom Dialectics</em> comes from that constant push and pull—between the quiet of an empty room and the chaos of the world outside, between wanting to understand how systems work and realizing how fragile our grip on them really is. I’m drawn to the quiet friction of ordinary life: the things we build, the habits we repeat without questioning, and the contradictions we live with every day.
    </p>

    <p>
      There is no publication schedule or desire to offer neat conclusions. Just an attempt to look closely at ordinary things, stay honest about what remains unresolved, and think through them one sentence at a time.
    </p>

    <h2>Colophon</h2>

